{"id": "851461cc-b128-482b-8ab5-1836bb3f7e1c", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T23:06:27.766889", "source": "user", "parent_id": "9cc9fa27-f2e5-4a58-ad60-5de0578c4277", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Plan acknowledged, but the framing needs adjustment before approval. Two issues to address before the main work begins. (1) The brief is design-only and the deliverable is a formal experiment design document, but the central research question and independent variables are mis-scoped. Performance benchmarks across algorithms are a secondary axis; the primary research question is what 'alphabetical' and 'word' mean concretely for this program and how each semantic choice (input contract, case handling, locale, tokenization, duplicate handling, output whitespace) determines correctness. Therefore: (a) articulate a clear research question / hypothesis at the top of the design (e.g., 'A Python word-sorting program satisfying a pre-registered input/output contract can be specified such that all candidate sort strategies agree on correctness across a defined edge-case battery, with performance variation across strategies quantified for tie-breaking'); (b) restructure the IV/DV model so that *semantic regimes* (case-sensitive vs lower vs casefold, LC_ALL=C vs locale-aware, strip-and-drop vs raw-split, stable vs dedup, single-line vs trailing-newline output) are the primary IVs, and *algorithm family* (sorted with key, cmp_to_key, locale.strxfrm) is a secondary IV crossed with the regimes. The DV is byte-equal correctness vs a pre-registered Python oracle (hard gate) plus wall-clock median of K trials (tie-break, descriptive only). (2) Pre-register the canonical contract inline in the design rather than burying each choice among assumptions: one numbered spec covering input format, token definition, ordering rule with a defined key function, duplicate handling, output whitespace including trailing newline, and locale. This spec is the single source of truth; deviations across regimes are what the experiment probes. Keep the assumptions and uncertainties lists but reorganise: anything that becomes part of the pre-registered spec moves out of 'assumptions'; U1–U10 remain open as design choices to be pinned down or held as ablation axes. Feasibility must explicitly state that execution is out of scope per the submission contract — describe intended harness (stdlib only, fixed seed, warm-up, N levels), not claimed results. A reasonable number of N levels for the described experiment is fine, but do not pre-commit to a single N=100,000 figure as if it were measured. When revising, do not run any code or fabricate timings; this remains design-only. After this reorganisation, the design should read as: research question → pre-registered contract → IV/DV matrix → procedure (harness spec, no execution) → controls (oracle construction, edge-case battery, reproducibility settings) → analysis plan (correctness-first, performance descriptive) → feasibility (stdlib only, Python 3.12.3, no network, sandboxed) → open choices and their fallback defaults. Resubmit the revised plan; I will then approve and request the full design synthesis.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"plan\", \"approach_approved\": false}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 12}
